1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,850
The telephone was an amazing invention.
电话是一项了不起的发明。

2
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:09,160
People could finally speak to each other instantly from one home to another.
人们终于能够足不出户，即刻与彼此对话。

3
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:18,480
The mobile phone took this technology further, meaning we could make contact with people to and from almost anywhere in the world.
移动电话则将这项技术推向了新高度，这意味着我们几乎能与世界各地的人随时取得联系。

4
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:27,040
But despite having the instant ability to talk to one another, it seems many of us prefer to communicate by text.
但即便拥有了即时通话的能力，我们中的许多人似乎还是更偏爱用短信沟通。

5
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:30,300
I don’t mean we have taken up letter writing again.
我并不是说大家又重拾了写信的习惯。

6
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:41,420
Although a handwritten letter has a personal touch, sending it by snail mail is too slow to satisfy our desire to keep in touch immediately.
虽说手写信件带有独特的个人温度，但平信邮寄的方式实在太慢，满足不了我们即时联络的需求。

7
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:50,960
But what our smartphones now allow us to do is enable us to communicate in any number of quick, cheap but silent ways.
而智能手机如今能让我们通过多种多样快捷、廉价又安静的方式进行沟通。

8
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:59,790
A study in the UK a few years ago found 49 per cent of teenagers preferred to send a text rather than speak to someone.
英国几年前的一项研究显示，49%的青少年更喜欢发短信，而非直接与人交谈。

9
00:00:59,790 --> 00:01:15,120
Certainly sending an SMS, text or message via a chat app means we can compose what we want to say, and we can be more efficient in saying it – especially if we express ourselves by using emojis.
毫无疑问，通过短信、文字消息或是聊天软件发送信息，能让我们斟酌好想要说的话，并且更高效地表达——尤其是当我们用表情符号辅助表达时。

10
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:26,040
It can also be quicker than having to chat with someone, although I feel you can still end up wasting time by having a full discussion by text!
这种方式通常也比实时通话更省时，不过我觉得，要是用短信来展开一次完整的讨论，最终还是会耗费不少时间！

11
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:35,390
Text messaging also allows privacy – other people can’t overhear your text if you’re in a public place such as on a train.
短信沟通还能保障隐私——当你身处火车之类的公共场所时，旁人不会听到你的聊天内容。

12
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:43,490
But texting instead of talking can seem antisocial; we may even forget the art of pleasantries.
但只发短信不与人交谈，会显得有些不合群，我们甚至可能会忘掉寒暄的社交艺术。

13
00:01:43,490 --> 00:01:49,940
A text can be misinterpreted too because you miss out on a speaker’s intonation.
短信内容也很容易被误解，因为你无法捕捉到说话人的语调。

14
00:01:49,940 --> 00:02:06,160
Neuroscientist Professor Sophie Scott told the BBC: “We always speak with melody and intonation to our voice… you take that channel of information out of communication, you lose another way that sense is being conveyed.”
神经科学家索菲·斯科特教授在接受BBC采访时表示：“我们说话时，声音里总会带有抑扬顿挫的语调……如果在沟通中去掉了这一信息传递渠道，就等于失去了另一种表达语义的方式。”

15
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:10,650
Of course texting and talking is the worst thing to do.
当然，一边发短信一边打电话是最不可取的行为。

16
00:02:10,650 --> 00:02:15,090
Who deserves your attention when two people are communicating with you?
当两个人同时在和你沟通时，你该把注意力放在谁身上呢？

17
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:20,200
It’s then we have to decide who has the most interesting thing to say!
到那时，你就得判断出谁的话更有意思了！
